The course-level end quiz got a module of its own,
\enquote{Repetitionsprov inför datorprovet}, right before the course's
summative computer exam.
It reveals the correct answers afterwards, as planned, and the cohort
uses that: the quiz doubles as practice for the exam, and its
description asks the students to take it before they start preparing,
which the preparation question checks.

\subsection{What the cohort's data can and cannot show}

The departures change what the analysis
(\cref{app:diagnostics-analysis}) can say about this cohort.
\begin{description}
\item[Level, not gain, for six topics] Functions and variables,
  conditionals, data types, repetitions and reading programs have only
  an after-measure, open from week~41, one to three weeks after their
  units; containers has only its after-measure, at the end of its
  unit.
  These measure the level the students had reached, not what the units
  added.
\item[Gain for two topics, with longer gaps] Classes and dictionaries
  and files have both measures, but their after-quizzes come later
  than planned: the classes after-quiz cannot be taken before its
  gateway module opens on 22 October, after the two-week examination
  period, and the files after-quiz sits in the revision module, so
  most students will take it after the week on graphical interfaces,
  when they start preparing for the computer exam.
  The time from teaching to after-quiz is recorded per submission and
  belongs in the analysis of these gains.
\item[No course-level gain] Without the start quiz, the course-level end
  quiz is a level measure only.
\item[Consent from the sibling study] The consent item was to open the
  course-level start quiz.
  Its wording is identical to that of the parallel debugging study's
  background quiz, \enquote{Diagnostic quiz: your programming
  background}, which the cohort answered in the first week of the
  Python part; for this cohort the analysis must therefore take consent
  from that quiz's report instead of the start quiz's.
\end{description}

The after-quizzes' description changed for the same reason as the
course-level one.
The planned description promises that the quiz mirrors the questions
from before the unit, which is false for the five catch-up quizzes; one
description serves all after-quizzes in Canvas, so all of them got a
description without the promise.

